3. What is the difference between a compiler and an interpreter? Is Java compiled or interpreted?

Java is both a compiled and an interpreted language.

A compiler translates source code into another form before execution, whereas an interpreter executes instructions at runtime.

In Java, the `javac` compiler converts the source code in a `.java` file into platform-independent bytecode, which is stored in a `.class` file.

At runtime, the JVM loads and executes this bytecode using an interpreter. It can also use the JIT compiler to convert frequently executed bytecode into native machine code to improve performance.

Therefore, Java uses both compilation and runtime execution techniques, which is why it is commonly described as a hybrid language.


